    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47415.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "Well, the first thing to say is when I came to Princeton in 2002 as a freshman, I had no clue what I wanted to do. And probably was headed towards that sort of track of, oh, you know, I'll go work in finance in New York or do consulting or something like that. I did know what I wanted to major in. I spent a year as a Latin American studies sort of pseudo major, which is really an excuse just to get a scholarship from the Spanish department to go work at an all inclusive resort in Mexico the summer after my freshman year, which is the greatest scam I ever pulled, possibly the greatest scam ever pulled in Princeton history. And then my sophomore year. After my sophomore year, I was studying abroad in England, studying at Cambridge, and then working for the Rand Corporation there, and then I studied in Spain. And I got assigned to this project studying terrorist targeting methods, both sort of ETA in Spain and then sort of Middle Eastern terrorist organizations. And it sounds foolish looking back, but at the time, I mean, it's the first time I started to think about, oh my gosh, we just invaded Iraq the year before. in Afghanistan when I was a freshman, you know, I started to ask these questions of why are we in the Middle East? What's going on there? Why are there these groups that want to destroy our way of life? And I just became fascinated",
