  {
    "episode_id": 47415,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47415.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "So I go to officer candidate school, I survived. And so if you survive officer candidate school, you earn your commission, but I basically had all of senior year to decide whether or not I wanted to accept my commission and do a three year commitment to the Supreme Court. It's always really weighing, weighing this, right? And there's no going back once you become a Marine. And I was at a dinner For the Woodrow Wilson School, the guest speaker was Richard Clark, who was the counter-terrorism guru for both Bush and Clinton. He wrote a book called Against All Enemies. He recently wrote a really good book on cyber issues, a good guy. And I happened to be seated next to him and the Dean of the Woodrow Wilson School and Marie Slaughter. And I'm like, well, I'm never going to have this opportunity again. So I'm like, well, hey, Mike Gallagher, a senior, I'm thinking about joining the Marine Corps. What advice would you have for me? And both of them advised me not to join the Marine and said something to the effect of like a Princeton education would be better spent at the CIA at worst. And I remember sort of being offended by that. And so there's a small part of Like, like, spite was a small reason I joined the Marine Corps just to prove that it could be done and that indeed it was a good use of a prince in education.",
