    "turn_idx": 43,
    "turn_text": "I had, when I got out of the Marine Corps, after seven great years, um, I went to work on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, worked for an amazing senator, Bob Corker, who was chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee, and I was his Middle East guy, and it was a fascinating time. And then I got an opportunity to work for Governor Walker, my home state governor, when he ran for president, I was his national security advisor and, um, just even though we didn't win, it was a phenomenal experience and he was a great guy to work for, just such a hard worker. I learned a lot from him. But there I was sort of sitting in Wisconsin. I knew I didn't want to go back to DC. I wanted to be sort of near my family. I wanted also to transition to the private sector and broaden my experience and not just sort of continue to do pure national security work, but connect that to the private sector. And so I really was just planning on, I was working for a great company in Green Bay called Break Your Fuel, and I was getting ready to teach adjunct at University of Wisconsin, Green Bay, because I used my GI bill to finish my PhD. And that was my plan. I was getting a private sector in academia, and maybe look for opportunities to serve in government should, you know, a Republican win at some point. But then my congressman unexpectedly retired. People thought he would have won easily for reelection. Great guy, Reid Ribble. But he unexpectedly retired, and so suddenly there was this opportunity to run for office. And I, my first reaction when some people reached out to me, see if I was interested was was hell no heck no sorry I know the",
