    "turn_text": "there at the Constitution convention he didn't write it in his notes but that's right But then because, you know, I wasn't the guy, even when I worked in a presidential campaign, you know, I was the guy writing white papers or draft foreign affairs articles. I wasn't in front of the camera. I had no experience with the fundraising side or anything having to do with the political side of the equation. So I thought this is just not, you know, I'm building this career as a national security professional and a private sector leader. And it just doesn't make sense. But then the more I thought of that, I'm like, you know, here I am writing articles, criticizing the direction of US foreign policy. criticizing the Obama administration's leadership. I worked for a campaign because I wanted to be part of fixing that. I need to put my money where my mouth is. And this is an opportunity to continue to serve my country in a different way, right? I can continue to work on national security issues. I feel passionate about and connect them to domestic issues as a legislator. And I also felt strongly that as a younger person, I think I was 31 at the time when I first announced and then turned 32 when I got elected, that we did need a new generation of leaders, particularly those who'd served in uniform who could bring that experience to the public, um, problems to public service and the biggest public policy issues of the day. So yeah, I kind of, it was right place, right time was not the favorite to win. The primary had a lot of support from the grassroots and just kind of built the thing, built the plane as it was taken off the runway. And here I am four years later.",
