  {
    "episode_id": 47415,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47415.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "But really the article was a result of a little bit of frustration as a freshman legislator and just observation about things that didn't make much sense to me. And let me down this path to try and study the history of Congress and how it's evolved or devolved as the case may be. And a few things became clear. I think conservatives have long said one of the fundamental problems with the government right now is that in deficiencies from a constitutional perspective or from a Madisonian perspective is that the first branch of government, Article 1, the legislative branch of government has surrendered a lot of its authority under the Constitution into the second branch of government, to the executive branch of government, which in turn allows the third branch of government to adjudicate a lot of issues that they really shouldn't be. adjudicating. And that's true. And I think that is probably the biggest problem we face. And I think it would surprise Madison, right? You know, and I think it would surprise all the authors of the Federalist Papers who indeed felt that the opposite might happen, right? That Congress would suck everything into its impetuous vortex, I",
