  {
    "episode_id": 47415,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47415.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 57,
    "turn_text": "phrase they used or that members of Congress being ambitious, power hunger people would never surrender power to the other branch. Right. Um, and you've all live in has written beautifully about this trend, but I think what is less understood is how dynamics within the institution have allowed that to happen. So in addition to that massive power shift from article one to article two, there's been a power shift from within Congress from the committees to house leadership and this started in the seventies. When all the Watergate babies got elected trying to clean up Washington and reform and ironically claw back some of their authority from and out of control executive branch. And they did a variety of things that I think were kind of productive. I think the War Powers resolution actually increased the power of the executive. the Budget Control and Empowerment Act created a highly dysfunctional budget process that increased the power of the executive. The National Emergencies Act, I think, was in 76. We recently had that controversy, also got me in trouble, increased the power of the executive. And then that was when you saw the creation of what is now referred to as the Steering Committee. And then the Republicans took that Congress in the 90s for the first time in a long time. They did the same thing, you know, they, they were elected to drain the swamp and they did a series of things that actually unintentionally increase the size of the swamp and increase the relative power of the executive branch. And so I sort of thought through, okay, this is a huge problem. It manifests itself in this spectacle, which you can see if you just turn on C-SPAN. where members of Congress are just giving speeches to an empty house chamber. We're never having an actual debate, you know, committee hearings unless they're about impeachment or something really juicy where you can get, you know, a clip on Fox News or MSNBC are sparsely attended or members parachute in and they read, you know, whatever their staff has prepared for them and then they parachute out and they continue fundraising. And I thought, well, this is a huge problem. And this is, and the final thing I'd say is, It also made me realize that I was wrong about my initial hypothesis about why Congress was dysfunctional and had lost the trust of the American people. I just thought prior to being elected that it was purely a people problem,",
