    "turn_text": "good and bad. Sure. The good is what I referenced before. It's that there actually are a lot of extremely talented, smart people in this institution. And honestly, in the last two elections, we've gotten waves on both sides of new freshmen, legislators, a lot of them have military experience and private sector experience that I think are going to be a much needed jolt of energy and intellectual honesty into the institution. Um, on the bad side, I would say the demands of fundraising for reelection are even more onerous than I imagined when I first ran and such that like, you know, when you first get elected, the advice you get from everyone is spend all your time, you know, focused on, on your reelection and it crowds out the time that you need to do oversight, right? I mean, I'm on the Armed Services Committee. It's probably the exception that proves the rule in terms of how committees don't work. Um, because it does work pretty well still. Um, thanks to great leadership from Thornberry and others. Uh, but you know, the Pentagon is a massive, massive bureaucracy and I, you know, I come to these issues with a certain amount of experience, probably a little bit more experience than your average member. You know, my, my graduate work is mostly on national security and defense issues, but it's tough. I mean, you got to spend a lot of time in order to understand the bureaucracy and conduct effective oversight and to legislate responsibly. And if you're spending all that time dialing for dollars, it's very hard to be an effective legislator. I know campaign finance issues are incredibly thorny from a constitutional perspective, and I don't pretend to be an expert on them, but the simple fact is that the time spent raising money is a huge problem for members of Congress.",
