    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "why I think in turn, the parties are weak because the attention is going towards all the bomb throwers and the insurgents and the people that want to burn the party and a variety of other institutions down. So I don't know if that aligns with Jeff's analysis, but I do concede the point that I think the party is in a weak position. I think Trump has also exposed that the perhaps what is referred to as like zombie Reaganism is not sufficient for the present moment. I would like, however, to see the Republican Party to return to some basic conservative principles. Perhaps foremost among them is fiscal conservatism. I get that word now in some sort of populist moment, but I think a country that doesn't maintain its fiscal house in order is a country that's destined to decline. And if the conservative party or the Republican party doesn't stand for fiscal conservatism, then what do we stand for? And I have to believe that we can be fiscally conservative while also being sensitive to the needs of working-class Americans. And I think, you know, we're never going to be able to outbid the progressives when it comes to the redistribution of other people's money. So that's point one that I'd like to see. Point two is, you know, in the past four years under President Trump, Even some progressives have suddenly rediscovered the virtues of states' rights and a federal system in",
