  {
    "episode_id": 47415,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47415.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 110,
    "turn_text": "So I was talked to my subordinates about Lombardi time and how important it is to show up early. And I know that sounds silly, but I think particularly in your 20s, like if you want to serve the country, if you want a career and policy, like you have a unique opportunity because you're not tied down by much. You don't need to buy a house. You shouldn't own anything that you can't put in the back of your car in less than 30 minutes. Like you can volunteer for every adventure, every opportunity to get out there, get your hands dirty, get your street cred. You can show up early for everything and you should never, you should, you really need to take advantage of that opportunity. And so that's one thing. I think the second thing that has really stuck with me for my time in the Marine Corps is this concept, which has now become a more popular concept. I think because of a few books that Simon Sinek has written, we would always say officers eat last. And that basic idea is that you have a duty as a leader to take care of your subordinates before you take care of yourself. You need to be concerned with their welfare before your own and you can't make what we refer to in the Marine Corps comfort base. decisions. Your priorities need to be the mission first, and your Marines second, and the more you take care of your Marines, the better chance you have of accomplishing the mission. And I think that concept is powerful, and I think it's applicable outside of military leadership. It's certainly something that I try to live out each day in Congress, albeit imperfectly, and I do feel like I have a duty to to help my constituents. This is not a platform for me to get famous or wealthy. It's a platform for me to serve people. And I've just found incidentally in my own life, and this is something I probably could have learned in 12 years of Catholic school, if I was paying better attention. The more I build my life around the idea of serving other people, the happier I tend to be. And the more I build my life around, satisfying my own sort of ego and ambitions or like, you know, things like wealth and power, the less happy I tend to be. And that's, I think that's a robust finding across many people's lives. The filing I'd say is, you know, what I struggled with in the Marine Corps is, particularly when you get to the fleet for the first time, is you should have this vision in your head of like military leadership and bearing and, you know, You can come off as very inauthentic if you're trying to be like the hardcore Arlie Ermey drill sergeant when that's not your natural leadership style.",
